class UnionFind
{
public:
    explicit UnionFind(std::size_t n);
    std::size_t find(std::size_t x);           // with path compression
    bool unite(std::size_t a, std::size_t b);  // union by size; false if already together
    bool connected(std::size_t a, std::size_t b);
    std::size_t sizeOf(std::size_t x);
    std::size_t components() const;
};

int countRegions(const std::vector<std::string>& grid);               // groups of '#' cells joined up, down, left or right
bool percolates(const std::vector<std::string>& grid);                // can water flow from the top row to the bottom row through '.' cells?
int firstCycle(std::size_t n, const std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, std::size_t>>& edges);   // index of the first edge that closes a cycle, or -1
```

- `find` and `unite` must be nearly O(1): use **union by size** and **path compression**. The tests run a million operations.
- `percolates`: water enters any open cell (`.`) of the top row and moves between open cells that share a side. Add two virtual nodes, "top" and "bottom", connected to every open cell of the first and last row. An empty grid does not percolate.
- `firstCycle`: add the edges in order; the first one whose ends are already connected closes a cycle.

